#!/usr/bin/env bash
# orch — single entrypoint for the tmux + Claude Code orchestrator.
set -euo pipefail
here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"

# --- inherited-ORCH_ROOT mismatch guard (issue #105) ------------------------------
# `orch` exports ORCH_ROOT, so every nested invocation (an _orch/*.sh script
# sourcing lib.sh, a worker's launch env, a test fixture's subshell) inherits
# it -- that inheritance is normal and must keep working. What is NOT normal is
# an ORCH_ROOT that was set by something OTHER than this same script's own
# $here on the way in: a leftover shell export, a worker's ambient launch env,
# `ORCH_ROOT=/tmp/D ./orch update` typed from install T. That already
# corrupted the live toolkit's .orch-version (stamped 9.9.9, which would have
# made `orch update` report "already up to date" forever), hijacked a
# self-referential fixture during #97, and nearly buried a real data-loss bug
# during #114 behind a false "cannot reproduce". Fail loudly on disagreement
# rather than silently acting on the wrong install, mirroring
# ensure_related_repo()'s ORCH_ALLOW_UNRELATED_REPO precedent in lib.sh.
#
# One legitimate case must not trip this: a hermetic bats test that
# deliberately points ORCH_ROOT at a throwaway scratch dir (same idiom as
# lib.sh's own issue #68 leak guard below) while running the real `orch`
# script, precisely so no state or file write can land in the real toolkit.
# That is the opposite of the danger here, so it is not a mismatch worth
# refusing over: detect it the same way #68 does, by checking whether
# ORCH_ROOT resolves under one of bats' own tmp roots. All three real
# incidents above were the other shape even when they happened during a bats
# run -- an AMBIENT ORCH_ROOT nobody deliberately isolated, pointing at a
# real, non-scratch toolkit -- so that shape still refuses under bats too.
if [ -n "${ORCH_ROOT:-}" ]; then
  # ORCH_ROOT need not exist yet (e.g. `orch help` never touches it) -- cd
  # canonicalizes when possible, and falls back to a plain trailing-slash
  # strip so a not-yet-created dir isn't treated as an automatic mismatch.
  resolved_orch_root="$(cd "$ORCH_ROOT" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || resolved_orch_root="${ORCH_ROOT%/}"
  orch_root_isolated=0
  if [ -n "${BATS_TEST_FILENAME:-}" ] && [ -n "$resolved_orch_root" ]; then
    for orch_bats_root in "${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR:-}" "${BATS_SUITE_TMPDIR:-}" "${BATS_RUN_TMPDIR:-}" "${BATS_TMPDIR:-}"; do
      [ -n "$orch_bats_root" ] || continue
      case "$resolved_orch_root" in
        "$orch_bats_root"|"$orch_bats_root"/*) orch_root_isolated=1; break ;;
      esac
    done
  fi
  # Same install, not just the same PHYSICAL PATH: comparing $resolved_orch_root
  # to $here byte-for-byte over-refuses on symlinks (orch invoked via a symlink
  # whose dirname differs from the real toolkit dir), /tmp vs /private/tmp-style
  # aliasing, and -- the case that actually broke this project's own worker
  # workflow -- two git worktrees of the SAME toolkit repo (this repo's workers
  # each run from their own `../wt/<hash>/<id>` worktree, per worktree_owner()
  # below, while often inheriting ORCH_ROOT pointing at the orchestrator's main
  # checkout). Fall back to repo-identity comparison, mirroring
  # ensure_related_repo()'s same-toplevel / shared-git-common-dir checks in
  # lib.sh (deliberately NOT its looser remote-URL/shared-history fallbacks --
  # those decide whether a TARGET project is "related enough" to a toolkit, a
  # weaker question than whether two paths ARE the same toolkit install).
  #
  # $here itself is `dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"`, which is NOT symlink-resolved
  # (bash's `cd` stays logical) -- so the actual symlink case above (orch
  # invoked as e.g. ~/bin/orch -> a real checkout) does not even reach $here
  # being "wrong", it reaches git-toplevel lookup FAILING outright, because
  # $here (~/bin) is often not inside any git repo at all. Resolve the
  # SCRIPT'S OWN symlink chain (not just its dirname) before the git-identity
  # fallback so that case is actually covered, not just documented as if it
  # were.
  orch_here_resolved="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
  orch_symlink_hops=0
  while [ -L "$orch_here_resolved" ]; do
    orch_symlink_hops=$((orch_symlink_hops + 1))
    # Bound the walk: a circular symlink chain (A -> B -> A) would otherwise
    # spin forever instead of falling back to whatever we've resolved so far.
    [ "$orch_symlink_hops" -le 40 ] || break
    orch_link_target="$(readlink "$orch_here_resolved")"
    case "$orch_link_target" in
      /*) orch_here_resolved="$orch_link_target" ;;
      *) orch_here_resolved="$(dirname "$orch_here_resolved")/$orch_link_target" ;;
    esac
  done
  # Fall back to the unresolved dirname (mirrors $resolved_orch_root's own
  # fallback above) rather than letting a dangling symlink's missing
  # directory abort the whole script via set -e with a bare `cd` error.
  orch_here_resolved="$(cd "$(dirname "$orch_here_resolved")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || orch_here_resolved="$(dirname "$orch_here_resolved")"
  unset orch_link_target orch_symlink_hops
  orch_root_same_install=0
  if [ "$resolved_orch_root" = "$here" ] || [ "$resolved_orch_root" = "$orch_here_resolved" ]; then
    orch_root_same_install=1
  elif [ -n "$resolved_orch_root" ] && command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    orch_here_top="$(git -C "$orch_here_resolved" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || orch_here_top=""
    orch_given_top="$(git -C "$resolved_orch_root" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || orch_given_top=""
    if [ -n "$orch_here_top" ] && [ -n "$orch_given_top" ]; then
      if [ "$orch_here_top" = "$orch_given_top" ]; then
        orch_root_same_install=1
      else
        orch_here_common="$(git -C "$orch_here_top" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || orch_here_common=""
        orch_given_common="$(git -C "$orch_given_top" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || orch_given_common=""
        [ -n "$orch_here_common" ] && [ "$orch_here_common" = "$orch_given_common" ] && orch_root_same_install=1
      fi
    fi
    unset orch_here_top orch_given_top orch_here_common orch_given_common
  fi
  if [ "$orch_root_same_install" -ne 1 ] && [ "$orch_root_isolated" -ne 1 ] \
      && [ "${ORCH_ALLOW_ROOT_MISMATCH:-0}" != "1" ]; then
    printf 'orch: inherited ORCH_ROOT (%s) does not match this script'"'"'s own location (%s).\n' \
      "$ORCH_ROOT" "$here" >&2
    printf 'orch: refusing to act on a different install than the one you ran -- unset ORCH_ROOT,\n' >&2
    printf 'or set ORCH_ALLOW_ROOT_MISMATCH=1 to override deliberately.\n' >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  unset resolved_orch_root orch_root_isolated orch_bats_root orch_root_same_install orch_here_resolved
fi
export ORCH_ROOT="${ORCH_ROOT:-$here}"

# --- dependency check (issue #20) -----------------------------------------------
# Previously only bootstrap.sh checked for required binaries, so a missing
# jq/tmux/perl surfaced as a cryptic failure mid-command instead of a clear
# message up front. Every command now goes through this entrypoint, so checking
# here catches it for all of them, not just `up`. Scoped to the binaries every
# command actually touches (jq for config/status, tmux for window ops, perl for
# strip_ansi) — `claude` and `git` are only needed by specific subcommands
# (up/spawn, spawn/collect) and are checked there instead, same as bootstrap.sh.
# lib.sh is sourced only if present alongside this script: some hermetic tests
# invoke a standalone copy of just this file (no sibling _orch/), exercising
# commands (logs/events/help) that need neither check_deps nor lib.sh's other
# helpers, so a missing lib.sh there must not be fatal.
if [ -f "$here/_orch/lib.sh" ]; then
  # shellcheck disable=SC1091
  source "$here/_orch/lib.sh"
  check_deps tmux jq perl
fi
# say() (issue #90, from lib.sh) is what every human-facing print below goes
# through. Genuinely reachable, not dead code: this is the SAME "standalone
# copy of just this file" mode documented above (the `if [ -f "$here/_orch/
# lib.sh" ]` guard at line 19) and already exercised by e.g.
# tests/orchlogs_cli.bats, which runs a copy of `orch` with no _orch/lib.sh
# beside it at all -- that test's assertions on the "no <name> log yet"
# messages (e.g. `orch logs watchdog` before any log exists) are what actually
# exercise this fallback; do not delete it thinking it's unreachable. That is
# an intentionally-supported partial deployment, not a broken checkout to
# mask — contrast _orch/send-remote-control.sh, which ships INSIDE the
# toolkit and always expects lib.sh right beside it; lib.sh missing there IS a
# broken checkout, and PR #89 removed that script's equivalent silent-degrade
# fallback for exactly that reason. Fall back to a bare, unstamped echo here
# so the logs/events/help commands standalone mode supports keep working
# without lib.sh.
#
# `declare -F`, not `command -v`: macOS ships a REAL BINARY at /usr/bin/say
# (the text-to-speech command). In standalone mode (lib.sh absent, the
# function below never defined), `command -v say` finds that binary and
# reports success — so the guard would conclude say is "already defined" and
# skip defining this fallback, leaving every call site below invoking the
# speech command with human-facing text as its argument instead of printing
# anything. `declare -F say` checks specifically for a shell FUNCTION named
# say, so it is unaffected by any same-named binary on PATH, on macOS or
# anywhere else.
declare -F say >/dev/null 2>&1 || say() { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }

# --- target-repo resolution (issue #35) ---------------------------------------
# The toolkit's own directory ($ORCH_ROOT) is NOT necessarily the repo workers
# should operate on (e.g. the toolkit vendored into another repo, or run from a
# scaffold). Resolve the target repo explicitly, most to least specific:
#   1. --repo <path>                    (this invocation only)
#   2. $PROJECT_ROOT                    (already set by the caller)
#   3. $ORCH_TARGET_REPO                (env, persists across invocations)
#   4. .target_repo in _orch/config.json (relative paths resolve against $ORCH_ROOT)
#   5. cwd                              (legacy default)
repo_flag=""
if [ "${1:-}" = "--repo" ]; then
  repo_flag="${2:?--repo needs a path}"
  shift 2
fi

if [ -n "$repo_flag" ]; then
  target_repo="$repo_flag"
elif [ -n "${PROJECT_ROOT:-}" ]; then
  target_repo="$PROJECT_ROOT"
elif [ -n "${ORCH_TARGET_REPO:-}" ]; then
  target_repo="$ORCH_TARGET_REPO"
else
  cfg_target="$(jq -r '.target_repo // empty' "$here/_orch/config.json" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [ -n "$cfg_target" ]; then
    case "$cfg_target" in
      /*) target_repo="$cfg_target" ;;
      *) target_repo="$here/$cfg_target" ;;
    esac
  else
    target_repo="$(pwd)"
  fi
fi

target_repo="$(cd "$target_repo" 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || {
  say "orch: target repo path does not exist: $target_repo" >&2
  exit 1
}
export PROJECT_ROOT="$target_repo"

# Default (issue #81): a per-toolkit-root name (see lib.sh's _orch_session_hash),
# so two installs never collide on the bare literal "orch". Falls back to the
# literal "orch" only when lib.sh wasn't sourced above (no sibling _orch/).
export SESSION_NAME="${SESSION_NAME:-orch}"

cmd="${1:-help}"; shift || true
case "$cmd" in
  up|bootstrap) exec "$here/_orch/bootstrap.sh" "$@" ;;
  spawn)        exec "$here/_orch/spawn.sh" "$@" ;;
  send)         exec "$here/_orch/send.sh" "$@" ;;
  status)
    json_mode=0
    if [ "${1:-}" = "--json" ]; then json_mode=1; shift || true; fi

    shopt -s nullglob
    files=("$here/_orch/state/workers"/*.json)

    # issue #70: ownership flag, keyed by the marker `orch attach`/`orch detach`
    # write/clear directly (see lib.sh's is_human_managed for the same convention).
    managed_by() { [ -f "$here/_orch/state/.manual-$1" ] && echo human || echo orch; } # <id>

    if [ "$json_mode" = 1 ]; then
      if [ "${#files[@]}" = 0 ]; then
        echo "[]"
        exit 0
      fi
      out="[]"
      for f in "${files[@]}"; do
        id="$(jq -r '.id // "?"' "$f")"
        managed="$(managed_by "$id")"
        row="$(jq -c --arg managed "$managed" '{id, status, model, task, created, updated, after, managed: $managed}' "$f")"
        out="$(jq -c --argjson row "$row" '. + [$row]' <<<"$out")"
      done
      printf '%s\n' "$out"
      exit 0
    fi

    # issue #90: the human table is many rows describing one snapshot in time,
    # not a log — stamping every row would be noise. Stamp the invocation once
    # instead (only when timestamps are enabled at all), so a scrollback paste
    # still shows when this `status` was run.
    if orch_timestamps_enabled; then say "orch status"; fi

    if [ "${#files[@]}" = 0 ]; then
      echo "no workers yet"
      exit 0
    fi

    # age (issue #17): elapsed time since `created` (falling back to `updated`
    # for status files written before this issue), rendered coarsely (Xs/Xm/XhYm/XdYh)
    # so the table stays scannable rather than exact-to-the-second.
    age_str() { # <iso8601-utc-ts>
      local ts="$1" epoch now secs
      [ -n "$ts" ] && [ "$ts" != "null" ] || { echo "?"; return; }
      epoch="$(date -u -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "?"; return; }
      now="$(date -u +%s)"
      secs=$((now - epoch))
      [ "$secs" -lt 0 ] && secs=0
      if [ "$secs" -lt 60 ]; then
        printf '%ds' "$secs"
      elif [ "$secs" -lt 3600 ]; then
        printf '%dm' $((secs / 60))
      elif [ "$secs" -lt 86400 ]; then
        printf '%dh%dm' $((secs / 3600)) $(((secs % 3600) / 60))
      else
        printf '%dd%dh' $((secs / 86400)) $(((secs % 86400) / 3600))
      fi
    }

    printf '%-12s %-8s %-12s %-8s %-8s %s\n' "ID" "MODEL" "STATUS" "AGE" "MANAGED" "TASK"
    for f in "${files[@]}"; do
      id="$(jq -r '.id // "?"' "$f")"
      model="$(jq -r '.model // "?"' "$f")"
      st="$(jq -r '.status // "?"' "$f")"
      ts="$(jq -r '.created // .updated // empty' "$f")"
      task="$(jq -r '.task // "" | gsub("\\s+"; " ")' "$f")"
      age="$(age_str "$ts")"
      managed="$(managed_by "$id")"
      printf '%-12s %-8s %-12s %-8s %-8s %s\n' "$id" "$model" "$st" "$age" "$managed" "$task"
    done

    # queue depth (issue #79): "a queue nobody can see is a queue nobody
    # maintains" — surface it here rather than requiring an operator to read
    # queue.jsonl by hand.
    queue_file="$here/_orch/state/queue.jsonl"
    if [ -s "$queue_file" ]; then
      printf 'queued: %s\n' "$(wc -l < "$queue_file" | tr -d ' ')"
    fi
    ;;
  collect)      exec "$here/_orch/collect.sh" "$@" ;;
  merge)        exec "$here/_orch/merge.sh" "$@" ;;
  ask)          exec "$here/_orch/ask.sh" "$@" ;;
  tail)
    command -v require_valid_session_name >/dev/null 2>&1 && require_valid_session_name
    id="${1:?usage: orch tail <id> [lines]}"; shift || true
    lines="${1:-15}"
    # session_exists() (issue #96): `tmux -t "$SESSION_NAME"` alone matches by
    # UNAMBIGUOUS PREFIX, so a bare list-windows here could silently read a
    # DIFFERENT, longer-named live session's windows instead of failing.
    # `declare -F`, not `command -v` (issue #98/#99): command -v also matches a
    # same-named BINARY on PATH, which would make this guard wrongly conclude
    # session_exists is defined. Same standalone-mode guard style as
    # require_valid_session_name just above.
    if ! declare -F session_exists >/dev/null 2>&1 || ! session_exists "$SESSION_NAME"; then
      say "no such tmux session: $SESSION_NAME" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    if ! tmux list-windows -t "$SESSION_NAME" -F '#{window_name}' 2>/dev/null | grep -qx "$id"; then
      say "no worker window '$id' in session '$SESSION_NAME'" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    pane_tail "$SESSION_NAME:$id" "$lines"
    ;;
  attach)
    command -v require_valid_session_name >/dev/null 2>&1 && require_valid_session_name
    # session_exists() (issue #96): same prefix-match hazard as `tail` above --
    # must not silently attach a human operator to a DIFFERENT live session.
    # `declare -F`, not `command -v` (issue #98/#99) -- see `tail` above.
    if ! declare -F session_exists >/dev/null 2>&1 || ! session_exists "$SESSION_NAME"; then
      say "no such tmux session: $SESSION_NAME" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    if [ -n "${1:-}" ]; then
      id="$1"
      if ! tmux list-windows -t "$SESSION_NAME" -F '#{window_name}' 2>/dev/null | grep -qx "$id"; then
        say "no worker window '$id' in session '$SESSION_NAME'" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
      # issue #70: taking manual control marks the worker human-managed BEFORE
      # attaching, so the orchestrator/watchdog stop driving it the instant a human
      # takes over, not only after they notice and type `orch detach`.
      command -v mark_human_managed >/dev/null 2>&1 && mark_human_managed "$id"
      exec tmux attach -t "$SESSION_NAME:$id"
    fi
    exec tmux attach -t "$SESSION_NAME"
    ;;
  detach)
    id="${1:?usage: orch detach <id>}"; shift || true
    # issue #70: hand a human-managed worker back to the orchestrator. Independent
    # of tmux attachment (Ctrl-b d) — this only clears the explicit ownership flag.
    command -v clear_human_managed >/dev/null 2>&1 && clear_human_managed "$id"
    state_dir="$ORCH_ROOT/_orch/state"
    mkdir -p "$state_dir"
    ev_line="$(jq -nc --arg id "$id" --arg ts "$(date -u +%FT%TZ)" '{id:$id, event:"detached", ts:$ts}')"
    printf '%s\n' "$ev_line" >> "$state_dir/inbox.jsonl"
    printf '%s\n' "$ev_line" >> "$state_dir/events.jsonl"
    say "detached $id (handed back to the orchestrator)"
    ;;
  down|stop)    exec "$here/_orch/stop.sh" "$@" ;;
  clean|kill)   exec "$here/_orch/clean.sh" "$@" ;;
  prune)        exec "$here/_orch/watchdog.sh" prune "$@" ;;
  remote-control) exec "$here/_orch/send-remote-control.sh" "$@" ;;
  update)       exec "$here/_orch/update.sh" "$@" ;;
  hud)          exec "$here/_orch/hud.sh" ;;
  logs)
    state_dir="$ORCH_ROOT/_orch/state"
    log_name="${1:-}"; shift || true
    case "$log_name" in
      heartbeat) log_file="$state_dir/heartbeat.log" ;;
      watchdog)  log_file="$state_dir/watchdog.log" ;;
      orch)      log_file="$state_dir/orch.log" ;;
      events)    log_file="$state_dir/events.jsonl" ;;
      *)
        say "orch logs: choose one of heartbeat|watchdog|orch|events" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    esac
    if [ ! -f "$log_file" ]; then
      say "no $log_name log yet ($log_file)"
      exit 0
    fi
    if [ "${1:-}" = "-f" ]; then
      exec tail -f "$log_file"
    fi
    tail -n "${ORCH_LOG_LINES:-200}" "$log_file"
    ;;
  events)
    events_file="$ORCH_ROOT/_orch/state/events.jsonl"
    if [ ! -f "$events_file" ]; then
      echo "no events recorded yet"
      exit 0
    fi
    printf '%-21s %-12s %s\n' "TIME" "ID" "EVENT"
    jq -r '[.ts, .id, .event] | @tsv' "$events_file" | \
      while IFS=$'\t' read -r ev_ts ev_id ev_event; do
        printf '%-21s %-12s %s\n' "$ev_ts" "$ev_id" "$ev_event"
      done
    ;;
  help|--help|-h|*)
    cat <<EOF
orch — tmux + Claude Code orchestrator

  ./orch [--repo <path>] <command> ...
                                     --repo pins the TARGET repo workers operate on,
                                     independent of where this toolkit lives. See
                                     "Target repo" below.

  ./orch up [--name <name>]          start session + master + heartbeat + watchdog
                                     (alias: bootstrap). --name sets AND PERSISTS
                                     this install's session name (issue #92) --
                                     see precedence below. Letters/digits/_/- only
                                     (tmux uses ':'/'.' as target separators).
  ./orch spawn <id> <model> "task" [--no-worktree] [perm]
                                     spawn a worker (own worktree + window)
                                     model: opus | sonnet | haiku
                                     [--no-worktree]        run in PROJECT_ROOT, no worktree
                                     [--continue]           resume newest session in that dir
                                     [--resume <id>]        resume a specific session id
                                     (--continue/--resume force --no-worktree: Claude keys
                                      sessions by project dir)
                                     [--after <id>]          hold this spawn queued until
                                     worker <id> reaches status "done", then auto-launch it
                                     perm (opt-in):
                                       --allow "cmd,cmd"  pre-approve commands
                                       --skip-permissions | --yolo
                                                           launch with claude
                                                           --dangerously-skip-permissions
                                                           (bypasses ALL permission checks;
                                                           trusted tasks in isolated
                                                           worktrees only). Requires
                                                           ORCH_ALLOW_SKIP_PERMISSIONS=1
                                                           in the environment.
  ./orch send  <id> "message"        message a running worker (one-way)
  ./orch tail  <id> [lines]          print a worker's recent pane output
                                     (default 15 lines)
  ./orch ask   <id> "question"       send a question, wait for the worker to
                                     finish answering, and print its reply
                                     (request/response; see ORCH_ASK_TIMEOUT
                                     and ORCH_ASK_LINES to tune the wait/output)
  ./orch clean <id> [--sweep-legacy] retire ONE worker: its tmux window, worktree
                                     ../wt/<hash>/<id>, branch orch/<hash>/<id>
                                     (<hash> namespaces the git layer per orch
                                     install, issue #86), status file and
                                     watchdog scratch (idempotent). --sweep-legacy
                                     also removes a pre-#86 ../wt/<id>/orch/<id>
                                     leftover for this id (opt-in: see clean.sh)
  ./orch kill  <id>                  alias for clean
  ./orch prune                       reap ALL terminal (done/spawn-failed)
                                     workers right now, ignoring retention —
                                     same teardown as clean, on demand
  ./orch status [--json]             list worker states, including "managed by"
                                     (orch|human, see \`attach\`/\`detach\` above)
  ./orch collect <id> [--base <br>]  emit one JSON object: branch diff
                                     (git diff <base>...orch/<hash>/<id>, default
                                     base: main) + the worker's status JSON —
                                     the uniform deliverable surface for
                                     review/merge flows
  ./orch merge <id> [--auto] [--base <br>]
                                     push orch/<hash>/<id>, open (or reuse) a PR against
                                     <br> (default: main). Without --auto (and
                                     merge.auto=false in config), only pushes +
                                     reports PR/check status — never merges. With
                                     --auto (or merge.auto=true): waits for
                                     required CI checks (merge.required_checks,
                                     merge.poll_interval_seconds,
                                     merge.timeout_seconds) and squash-merges +
                                     deletes the branch only if every required
                                     check is SUCCESS. Refuses to merge a
                                     conflicting/dirty PR. On block/timeout,
                                     marks the worker "blocked" and emits a
                                     merge-blocked event (see "orch events");
                                     on success, marks it "merged" and emits
                                     merged.
  ./orch attach [<id>]               attach to the tmux session, or jump straight
                                     to one worker's window. \`attach <id>\` also
                                     marks that worker human-managed (issue #70):
                                     the orchestrator/watchdog stop driving it
                                     until \`orch detach <id>\`.
  ./orch detach <id>                 hand a human-managed worker back to the
                                     orchestrator (clears the flag \`attach <id>\`
                                     set; emits a "detached" event). NOT the same
                                     as tmux-detaching the terminal (Ctrl-b d) —
                                     that flag is explicit state, independent of
                                     terminal attachment.
  ./orch down                        stop background loops (session stays up)
                                     (alias: stop)
  ./orch hud                         print the one-line status HUD
  ./orch logs <heartbeat|watchdog|orch|events> [-f]
                                     view a log (-f to follow); events shows the
                                     raw durable event history (see "orch events")
  ./orch events                      formatted view of the durable event history
                                     (every done/needs-input/stalled/spawn-failed
                                     event, surviving the inbox's destructive drain)
  ./orch update [--check] [--force]  self-update this vendored copy from upstream
                                     (issue #91). Compares .orch-version/VERSION
                                     against upstream's VERSION file and applies
                                     via install.sh (preserves your _orch/config.json).
                                     --check reports what would change without
                                     applying. Refuses to apply (rewriting
                                     _orch/*.sh out from under a live heartbeat/
                                     watchdog loop or live workers is unsafe)
                                     while any worker is live or the tmux session
                                     is up, unless --force — restart the loops
                                     yourself afterward (\`./orch down && ./orch up\`).
                                     A throttled daily background check (at most
                                     one network call per 24h, see update.* in
                                     _orch/config.json) runs from the heartbeat
                                     and only notifies ("update available: X -> Y,
                                     run \`orch update\`") — it never auto-applies.
  ./orch remote-control [--force] [<session:window>]
                                     type /remote-control into the ORCHESTRATOR's
                                     own pane and submit it (keepalive/reconnect
                                     nudge, e.g. from cron). Defaults to
                                     \$SESSION_NAME:\$ORCH_WINDOW; skips a pane with
                                     an unsent draft unless --force. The session
                                     name in <session:window> must be EXACT
                                     (issue #96) -- an abbreviated prefix that tmux
                                     would resolve on your behalf is refused rather
                                     than silently driving a different, live
                                     session.

Session name precedence (issue #92): --name flag (orch up, persists to
_orch/state/session-name) > \$SESSION_NAME env (this invocation only, does not
persist) > persisted name from a prior --name > orch-<hash of this toolkit's
resolved root> default (issue #81, so two installs in different directories
never collide on one tmux session). Renaming via --name while the OLD
persisted session is still running is refused, not silently orphaned — stop
it first or finish up under the old name.

Env overrides: SESSION_NAME (see precedence above), PROJECT_ROOT (default: cwd)

Terminal output timestamps (issue #90): every human-facing message this CLI and
its background loops print is prefixed with a timestamp by default (never
machine-readable output like status --json or collect's JSON). Disable with
ORCH_TIMESTAMPS=0 or output.timestamps=false in _orch/config.json (env wins);
pick the stamp format with ORCH_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT=iso|short or
output.timestamp_format in config.json (default: iso, matching orch.log's UTC
stamp — best for correlating a terminal line with its orch.log entry; short is
a local HH:MM:SS clock, best for reading a live-attached session at a glance).

Target repo (issue #35): resolved as --repo flag > \$PROJECT_ROOT > \$ORCH_TARGET_REPO
> .target_repo in _orch/config.json > cwd. Workers' worktrees are always created
against the resolved target repo, never inferred from where this toolkit lives.
If the toolkit dir and the resolved target repo share no git history or remote,
spawn refuses to run (set ORCH_ALLOW_UNRELATED_REPO=1 to override intentionally).
To update a vendored copy of this toolkit, run \`./orch update\` (see above) — do
not \`git pull\` inside the target repo expecting toolkit changes; by design its
history is unrelated to the toolkit's own.

Spawns are refused-and-queued (not dropped) when at capacity: concurrency
(thresholds.max_workers), free memory (thresholds.min_free_mb/est_worker_mb), or
estimated spend (budget.max_usd) in _orch/config.json. The heartbeat drains the
queue once a worker frees a slot.

Task dependencies (issue #22): 'orch spawn <id> ... --after <dep-id>' always
queues <id> (regardless of capacity) and holds it until worker <dep-id> reaches
status "done"; the heartbeat auto-launches it then, same drain loop as above.
